Permacomputing is a way of practicing computing that takes its ethics from [[Permaculture]]: care for the earth, care for people. It reuses the hardware and software that already exist, keeps things small enough to understand, and treats limits as material to design with. The community and its principles live at https://permacomputing.net/.

Hannah Gmeiner's bachelor's thesis, //Permacomputing as a Practice for Digital Graphic Design//, tries those principles out on graphic design. Tim Rodenbroeker interviewed her about it in August 2024.

* Thesis: https://permacomputingasapracticefordigitalgraphicdesign.com/
* Interview: https://trcc.timrodenbroeker.de/hannah-gmeiner/

She sums the principles up as an approach that is "as transparent, resource-efficient, low-complexity, sustainable, and flexible as possible."

! Maximalist techno-aesthetics

This is the permacomputing name for what it argues against.

<<<
This describes a mindset in which quantitative growth is seen as good and desirable. It glorifies a data-intensive reality, increasing complexity, and growing resource consumption by digital devices.
<<< Hannah Gmeiner

Her example is social media moving from text posts to image posts to video posts. For designers it shows up as steady pressure to adopt each new tool, and as a reluctance to criticize how the new tools look.

It gives [[High Fidelity]] a motive. See [[Why are lo-fi things appealing?]]

Its companion is [[Virtualism]]: hiding how the system works so the growth is easy to live with.

! Two kinds of limits

A self-imposed limit is one you choose: a format, a rule, an old machine. She says these "can function as amplifiers of creativity while simultaneously focusing attention on the process behind the work."

An externally imposed limit comes from outside the work: restrictive copyright law, uncertain legal status, no money, no access to a gallery. Less privileged artists adapt to these whether they want to or not.

The first kind is where [[Radical Simplicity]] and [[Low Fidelity]] sit. The second kind is closer to [[The Libre Designer]].

! Making limits visible

"Integrating and making boundaries visible in the process plays a significant role." That is [[Signs Signaling Sustainability]] in one sentence. [[Solar.LowTechMagazine.com]] is the example I already have.

! Nostalgia

Using an old machine gets called nostalgic because the machine has run out of economic value. Permacomputing answers that there is no better era of computing to miss.

<<<
From the perspective of Permacomputing, it is impossible to long for and romanticize a time when computer technology was better because such a time never existed.
<<< Hannah Gmeiner

What she wants in its place is "a more reflective nostalgia, within which experimenting, playful combination, and critical thinking occur." The end of a computer's economic life is when somebody can reclaim it.

! What carried over to graphic design, and what didn't

* Saving and reusing resources worked well.
* Reusing old devices worked less well. She hit technical hurdles she couldn't clear alone.
* "One resource you definitely don't save on is time."
* "Not all principles could be transferred because they were too technically loaded."
* She couldn't plan an exact output. The process had to stay experimental.

! Scaling down

<<<
Scalability is also a crucial design variable that requires a compelling rationale when expanding within the context of Permacomputing. Often, scalability downwards is more significant.
<<< Hannah Gmeiner

Scaling up needs a reason. Scaling down usually matters more. Not written up yet. Cases to write it from: the image list on a.wjerk.shop, the 800px dithered tiles, the cutter server on a Pi, and exercise 10 in [[Permacomputing Exercises]].

! The experiments

The thesis site documents each one, with the file size printed beside every image.

|!Experiment |!What she did |!Tools |
|EPUB poster |A poster built as an EPUB in HTML and CSS, shown on a second-hand Kindle. She filmed the e-ink flicker between pages as animation. |VS Code, Kindle 6th gen |
|Cross-stitch type |Letters drawn on an 8-pixel grid, stitched, then scanned. |Needle, thread, scanner |
|Reused material |Discarded prints collected from beside the university printers, then scanned, enlarged, and recombined into posters. |Scanner |
|Digital diary |Mastodon posts about permacomputing typed into a used Casio organiser with 128 KB of storage. |Casio digital diary |
|Small-file poster |Posters designed at 24 × 34 px, mixing vector and pixel elements. The PDFs stay under 600 KB and print legibly at A3. |Vector program |
|Compression artefacts |Classmates' videos compressed until the data structure shows. |~HandBrake |
|Text only |Posters and animations made in a plain text editor with ASCII art, mostly under 10 KB. |Text editor |
|LED panel |Processing sketches on a 64 × 64 px panel, red and green LEDs only. |Processing |

I turned these into [[Permacomputing Exercises]].

! Her own objection

She raises the charge against permacomputing herself. Its limits are chosen. The people practicing it are not disadvantaged by geography or money, and nothing forces them.

<<<
permacomputing requires thorough consideration to prevent it from becoming a hobby for the privileged or glorifying and aestheticizing poverty.
<<< Hannah Gmeiner

She adds that this "should in no way be understood as an excuse for extractivism and wasteful practices."

This is maybe everything that I do?

